commit: 869411c8276c7ca0290f684137570eb7b0712c3d

{"body":"Part of t-393, acceptance fixes. This task adds the comma design's item S6 asks for in the Schedule footnote.\n\n### What changed\nThe footnote now reads \"Next: Friday, 20 November.\" in British English. `MedicationScheduleLine.footnote` takes the day from `DayDateText.text` and adds a comma after the weekday only when the language's own pattern has none. It finds the weekday by formatting it for the same locale and inserts the comma only when a space follows. Where the pattern already writes a comma, the day is left as it is, so American English (\"Friday, November 13\") and Russian (\"Пятница, 13 ноября\") print one comma and not two. A pattern that does not start with the weekday, or does not separate it by a space, is left alone. `DayDateText` is untouched, so Today's date line and History's day headers keep their format. The catalog comment for the sentence now shows the comma in its example. Every Russian value is unchanged.\n\n### Starts and Ends pills\nStarts and Ends are the system compact DatePicker, so the system format stays, and design changes the board. Nothing in `App/Schedule` changed in this diff.\n\n### Walk\nOn a simulator that stays en_US, `schedule-footnote` read \"Every 2 months on day 3 at 09:00. Next: Sunday, January 3.\", one comma, as the American case needs. Launch arguments for en_GB and ru did not change that simulator's locale, so those two are proved by the unit tests and not on screen. Today shows \"Tuesday, November 17\" and History's headers \"Sunday, November 15\" and \"Thursday, November 12\". Those commas come from the en_US system pattern, as before, since `DayDateText` is not in the diff.\n\n### Shared pieces\nNo shared piece fits. The change touches no UI code, only text built in `AnkyDomain`.\n\n### Look check\nThe brief names no design picture. I checked by eye that the footnote text under the Schedule card reads as one sentence with one comma after the weekday. No control changed.\n"}
